@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ca-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `ca-interpretation-repeal`
IMPORT `ca-interpretation-resolutions`

-- ca-tests-resolutions.l4
--
-- Section 39(1): the four expressions, which body each names, what each requires, and what a resolution does (the resolutions module).
-- Expected values written from the text (lines 376 to 379 and 381) before the module was run.
-- The "#" numbers are the scenario numbers of the independent test pass whose questions led to the module (NOTES.md, section 8).

§ `Section 39(1): the body a regulation is laid before`

-- (a) and (c) say "laid before Parliament"; (b) and (d) say "laid before the House of Commons".
#ASSERT `the body before which a regulation must be laid under` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` EQUALS `Parliament`
#ASSERT `the body before which a regulation must be laid under` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` EQUALS `the House of Commons`
#ASSERT `the body before which a regulation must be laid under` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` EQUALS `Parliament`
#ASSERT `the body before which a regulation must be laid under` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` EQUALS `the House of Commons`

§ `Section 39(1): the fifteen days for laying are the same for all four expressions`

-- The four paragraphs use the same words for the time ("within fifteen days after it is made or, if [the body] is not then sitting, on any of
-- the first fifteen days next thereafter that [it] is sitting"), so the time module's function serves (c) and (d) as it serves (a) and (b).
-- Made on Monday 9 March 2026, a sitting day: the last day is 24 March, and 25 March is too late.
#ASSERT `the last day for laying a regulation made on` (YMD 2026 3 9) `given the days the body sits` (GIVEN d YIELD NOT (`is weekend` d)) EQUALS YMD 2026 3 24
#ASSERT `the regulation made on` (YMD 2026 3 9) `may be laid on` (YMD 2026 3 24) `given the days the body sits` (GIVEN d YIELD NOT (`is weekend` d))
#ASSERT NOT (`the regulation made on` (YMD 2026 3 9) `may be laid on` (YMD 2026 3 25) `given the days the body sits` (GIVEN d YIELD NOT (`is weekend` d)))

§ `Section 39(1): which resolutions each expression names`

-- (a) and (c): "a resolution of both Houses of Parliament": the Senate and the House of Commons each pass one.
-- The Senate has not passed one, the House of Commons has (#569 and #573: one House is not both).
#ASSERT NOT (`the resolutions that the expression` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`the resolutions that the expression` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE)
-- The Senate has, the House of Commons has not.
#ASSERT NOT (`the resolutions that the expression` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- Both have.
#ASSERT `the resolutions that the expression` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
#ASSERT `the resolutions that the expression` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
-- Neither has.
#ASSERT NOT (`the resolutions that the expression` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- (b) and (d): "a resolution of the House of Commons": the House of Commons alone; the paragraph does not mention the Senate.
#ASSERT `the resolutions that the expression` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
#ASSERT `the resolutions that the expression` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
#ASSERT `the resolutions that the expression` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`the resolutions that the expression` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` `names have been passed, given that the Senate has passed one is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)

§ `Section 39(1)(a) and (b): coming into force depends on affirmation`

-- (a) and (b) say the regulation "shall not come into force unless and until it is affirmed"; (c) and (d) say nothing of the kind.
#ASSERT `the coming into force of a regulation depends on a resolution if it is` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament`
#ASSERT `the coming into force of a regulation depends on a resolution if it is` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons`
#ASSERT NOT (`the coming into force of a regulation depends on a resolution if it is` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the coming into force of a regulation depends on a resolution if it is` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons`)
-- (a): affirmed by the House of Commons only: not affirmed as (a) requires, so it cannot have come into force (#569).
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE)
-- (a): affirmed by both Houses: the bar is lifted.
#ASSERT `a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
-- (a): affirmed by neither.
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- (b): affirmed by the House of Commons: the bar is lifted, whatever the Senate has done (#571).
#ASSERT `a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
-- (b): not affirmed by the House of Commons, though the Senate has affirmed it: still barred.
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- (c) and (d): no condition on coming into force, so section 39(1) leaves the way open with no resolution passed (#575, first piece).
#ASSERT `a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE
#ASSERT `a regulation can have come into force as far as section 39(1) is concerned, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an affirming resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE

§ `Section 39(1)(c) and (d): annulment`

-- (c): annulled by a resolution of both Houses (#572).
#ASSERT `a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
-- (c): a resolution of the House of Commons alone does not annul it (#573): the paragraph names a resolution of both Houses and "means" no more.
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- (d): annulled by a resolution of the House of Commons alone (#574).
#ASSERT `a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE
-- (d): a resolution of the Senate alone is not what the paragraph names.
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- Never annulled (#575, second piece): no resolution, so it is not annulled.
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to negative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` FALSE)
-- "Remains in force unless annulled" (#575) is not said in these words by section 39(1)(c) or (d), and the encoding has no one function that says it.
-- It is composed from the two pieces above and from section 39(2): with no annulment section 39(2) revokes nothing, and the way to coming into force is open.
-- With an annulment on 2026-06-10 (both Houses, under (c)), section 39(2) deems the regulation revoked on that day (the repeal module): in force the day before, not the day after.
#ASSERT `a regulation annulled by a resolution passed on` (YMD 2026 6 10) `is in force on` (YMD 2026 6 9)
#ASSERT NOT (`a regulation annulled by a resolution passed on` (YMD 2026 6 10) `is in force on` (YMD 2026 6 11))
-- Paragraphs (a) and (b) say nothing of annulment, so the Act does not answer for an affirmative expression.
#ASSERT REFUSED (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of Parliament` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` TRUE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE)
#ASSERT REFUSED (`a regulation is annulled, being subject to` `subject to affirmative resolution of the House of Commons` `where the Senate has passed an annulling resolution is` FALSE `and the House of Commons has passed one is` TRUE)
